.TH PARREAD 2
.SH NAME
parread \- read a buffer as several reads in flight at once
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "parread.m";
parread := load Parread Parread->PATH;

pread: fn(fd: ref Sys->FD, b: array of byte, off: big, ways: int): (int, string);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Pread
fills
.I b
from offset
.I off
of
.I fd
as up to
.I ways
reads at once, each a
.B sys->pread
loop over a contiguous piece of
.I b
in a process of its own.
Over a
.IR mount (1)
each read is a 9P request of its own, waiting a round trip for its answer;
several overlap their waits where one read's requests would follow each
other.
It is for large reads at known offsets: a video feed's frames
(see
.IR vid9p (4)),
read whole at
.IR n *frame-size.
.PP
It returns the number of bytes read contiguously from
.IR off ,
a piece that came back short ending the count, and the first error in the
order of the pieces
.RB ( nil
if none).
So a read past the end of the file gives the bytes before it and no
error, and a refused piece gives its error and the bytes before it, as one
.B pread
loop would.
With
.I ways
1 or less, or a buffer too small to be worth sharing (under 16K a piece),
it is that one loop.
.SH EXAMPLES
A 720p frame from a Raspberry Pi 5's feed, read by a Raspberry Pi 4 over
an encrypted mount at 32512-byte messages, took 67 ms in one read and 39 ms
in two or more: the connection's decryption is then the limit.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/parread.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sys-read (2),
.IR vid9p (4)
